  "ch1.leg.ni": "Intrinsic density nᵢ",
  "ch1.say.intrinsic": "Undoped: electrons and holes are equal in number, both <em>{ni}</em> per cm³, all from thermal excitation. A cubic centimetre of silicon holds 5×10²² atoms, so on average there is one free electron for every {per} atoms.",
  "ch1.say.extrinsic.n": "With {N} per cm³ of phosphorus, on average one impurity atom sits among every {per} silicon atoms. Yet the electron density already equals the impurity density, {x} times the intrinsic density of {ni}. Who carries the current is your choice, not the temperature's.",
  "ch1.say.extrinsic.p": "With {N} per cm³ of boron, on average one impurity atom sits among every {per} silicon atoms. Yet the hole density already equals the impurity density, {x} times the intrinsic density of {ni}. Who carries the current is your choice, not the temperature's.",
  "ch1.say.near": "At {T} K the temperature is close to this silicon's “intrinsic temperature” of {Ti} K. Thermally generated carriers are about to match what the impurities supply, and the doping is starting to lose control.",
  "ch1.say.hot": "At {T} K the temperature is above the intrinsic temperature of {Ti} K. Thermal excitation now makes more electrons and holes than the impurities do, and the difference between n-type and p-type is disappearing. At this temperature a chip is no longer a chip.",
  "ch1.p3": "At 300 K the model gives an intrinsic density of 9.8×10⁹ per cm³ (measured values in the literature lie between 9.65×10⁹ and 1.0×10¹⁰). Pure silicon's resistivity at this point is about 3.5×10⁵ Ω·cm. Add 10¹⁶ per cm³ of phosphorus, about one dopant atom for every 5 million silicon atoms, and the resistivity falls to 0.56 Ω·cm, <strong>a drop of about 600,000 times</strong>. That is what semiconductor means: whether a material conducts is decided by a very small amount of impurity, placed where you want it.",
  "ch1.p4": "Temperature is the other lever. Near room temperature, every rise of about 9 K doubles the intrinsic density. In silicon doped at 10¹⁶, thermal carriers match the dopant supply at about 649 K (about 376 °C), and past that point the doping no longer controls anything. Chips run far below this, but the exponential comes back in chapter 3 in the form of leakage.",
  "ch1.limit": "Simplifications: all impurities are assumed to be ionised (not true below about 100 K); band-gap narrowing at heavy doping is ignored; mobility is the empirical Caughey–Thomas (1967) fit at 300 K and does not vary with temperature; the band gap E<sub>g</sub>(T) uses the Varshni formula. N<sub>c</sub> and N<sub>v</sub> are taken at 300 K as 2.8×10¹⁹ and 2.65×10¹⁹ per cm³. Textbooks and databases do not agree on these two numbers: the Ioffe Institute database gives 3.2×10¹⁹ and 1.8×10¹⁹, which put into the same formula give 8.6×10⁹. The pair used here is the one that places the intrinsic density inside the measured range.",

  /* ───── 02 ───── */
  "ch2.title": "The pn Junction: A Gate That Opens One Way",
  "ch2.law": "Join p-type and n-type silicon and a voltage wall forms at the boundary by itself. Each rise of about 60 millivolts in the forward bias multiplies the current crossing the wall by ten.",
  "ch2.p1": "The n-type side has many electrons, the p-type side has many holes. Once they are joined, electrons diffuse into the p region and holes diffuse into the n region, and they cancel each other at the boundary. That leaves a layer with no free carriers, only fixed charged impurity ions, called the <strong>depletion region</strong>. These ions create an electric field that pushes later carriers back, until diffusion and field balance. The voltage that corresponds to this field is the <strong>built-in potential</strong> V<sub>bi</sub> = (kT/q)·ln(N<sub>A</sub>N<sub>D</sub>/n<sub>i</sub>²), which brings back n<sub>i</sub> from chapter 1.",
  "ch2.p2": "Apply a forward voltage and the wall gets lower, and the carriers that get over it grow as the Boltzmann factor e<sup>qV/kT</sup>. Apply a reverse voltage and the wall gets higher, and almost no current flows. This is the <strong>Shockley diode equation</strong> I = I<sub>s</sub>(e<sup>qV/kT</sup> − 1). To picture the field in the depletion region, compare it with the page on <a href=\"../../../physics/electromagnetism/electric-fields/\">Electric Fields</a>.",
  "ch2.model.title": "A Silicon pn Junction",
  "ch2.model.how": "Drag the doping on each side and the applied voltage (positive is forward). The upper plot is the band energy across the junction, with the depletion region in grey. The lower plot is the absolute value of the current against voltage (log scale), and the gold dot marks the present voltage.",
  "ch2.model.aria": "Above: pn-junction band energies and depletion region. Below: log plot of diode current against voltage",
  "ch2.ctl.na": "p-side doping N<sub>A</sub>",
  "ch2.ctl.nd": "n-side doping N<sub>D</sub>",
  "ch2.ctl.v": "Applied voltage",
  "ch2.read.vbi": "Built-in potential",
  "ch2.read.w": "Depletion width",
  "ch2.read.e": "Peak field",
