  "ch4.leg.data": "Real processors (compiled by Rupp)",
  "ch4.say.start": "Generation 0: the starting point, size {L}, voltage {V} V. Drag the generation to the right to see how power per unit area changes.",
  "ch4.say.flat": "Generation {g}, size {L}: the voltage has shrunk with it to {V} V, and power per unit area is {x} times that of generation 0, nearly unchanged. This is the “free lunch” of Dennard scaling.",
  "ch4.say.leak": "Generation {g}, size {L}: the threshold has dropped to {vt} V, leakage is already {share} of total power, and power density is {x} times higher. If you keep shrinking by Dennard's table, switches that can no longer turn off will burn the chip.",
  "ch4.say.hot": "Generation {g}, size {L}: the voltage did not shrink with it, and power per unit area has risen to {x} times. With chip area unchanged, heat removal would have to rise by the same factor, which cannot be done.",
  "ch4.say.fixedcool": "Generation {g}, size {L}: the voltage did not shrink. Even if the clock does not rise, power per unit area is {x} times that of generation 0, because more capacitance is packed into the same area.",
  "ch4.say.stall": "Generation {g}, size {L}: the voltage has stopped at {V} V and no longer falls, yet the clock keeps rising, and power density is {x} times higher. This is the “power wall” that processors ran into around 2005.",
  "ch4.say.cool": "Generation {g}, size {L}: the clock no longer rises. Power density is {x} times that of generation 0, and leakage is {share} of the total. The cost: the extra transistors no longer make a single core faster, so they become more cores.",
  "ch4.say.realleak": "Generation {g}, size {L}: the clock no longer rises, and switching power is held down. But leakage grows with the number of transistors and has reached {share}, so power density is {x} times that of generation 0. Real processes push this down with high-κ gate dielectrics and FinFETs, which the model does not include.",
  "ch4.p3": "Choose “Dennard scaling” and let the clock rise, and power density stays flat for the first three generations. But the threshold is also shrinking. By generation 5 (about 180 nm), leakage makes up 63% of total power and power density is 2.7 times higher; by generation 6 it is 10.9 times. Choose “voltage held fixed” and, as the clock rises, power density doubles every generation, reaching 256 times at generation 8.",
  "ch4.p4": "What actually happened was the third strategy. Voltage shrank to about 1 V and stopped there, with the threshold staying near 0.3 V. If the clock still rose, power density at generation 8 would be 10.7 times higher; if the clock did not rise, it stays roughly at its original level. The real data in the plot below is the trace this choice left behind. In this data, the highest clock of processors before 2000 was under 540 MHz, it reached 3.65 GHz in 2000–2005, and from then until 2021 the top clock was only 4.7 GHz. The industry turned the extra transistors into several cores, larger caches and dedicated circuits. The term “dark silicon” (transistors that cannot all be powered on at once) appeared at this time.",
  "ch4.limit": "The model's generation 0 uses 1 µm, 5 V, a threshold of 0.75 V and 25 MHz. These are round integers chosen to set the ratio of leakage to switching power at the starting point; they are not the specifications of any chip. “Shrink to 1 V, then stop” is a schematic of history, not generation-by-generation data. The frequency data come from Karl Rupp's 50 Years of Microprocessor Trend Data (CC BY 4.0; data before 2010 were collected by Horowitz and colleagues), with each point being one processor. High-κ metal gates, FinFETs and the other measures added after 2007 to hold down leakage are not in the model.",

  /* ───── 05 ───── */
  "ch5.title": "Printing Lines Finer Than the Light That Prints Them",
  "ch5.law": "The finest line a set of optics can print is k₁·λ/NA. The physical floor for k₁ is 0.25, so to go finer you must use a shorter wavelength, a larger numerical aperture, or split one layer's pattern across several exposures.",
  "ch5.p1": "Chip patterns are made by a kind of photography: light passes through a mask carrying the pattern, a lens reduces it fourfold, and it is projected onto silicon coated with photoresist. Where the light lands, the resist changes, and after development the pattern remains. The problem is that light diffracts. A row of evenly spaced lines (period P) splits the light into beams whose directions satisfy sinθ = mλ/P, where m is the diffraction order. This is the same effect as <a href=\"../../../physics/waves/interference/\">Interference</a> and <a href=\"../../../physics/modern/double-slit/\">The Double-Slit Experiment</a>.",
  "ch5.p2": "The lens collects only light whose sinθ is no larger than the <strong>numerical aperture</strong> NA. If it collects only the zeroth order, the image is a uniform glow and nothing prints. At least one first-order beam must also be collected, so that two beams interfere and bright and dark fringes appear. This is Abbe's theory of imaging, and it is the origin of Rayleigh's formula CD = k₁·λ/NA. With oblique illumination, the zeroth and first orders can be placed on opposite sides of the pupil, and the smallest period can then reach λ/(2NA), that is, a half-pitch with k₁ = 0.25.",
  "ch5.model.title": "The Aerial Image of a Set of Evenly Spaced Lines",
  "ch5.model.how": "Choose the lithography tool and the illumination, drag the line period you want to print, and decide how many exposures to split it into. The upper plot shows the light intensity from one exposure on the silicon (Abbe imaging, summing over each point of the source), with the red dashed line marking the development threshold. The lower plot shows the lines that finally remain on the silicon, with different colours for different exposures. When split into several exposures, each one keeps only a line as wide as the target linewidth (with the threshold raised to match), and the exposures are offset and stacked together.",
  "ch5.model.aria": "Above: light intensity from the mask lines after the projection lens. Below: the lines finally printed",
